id: core/co-orientation-loop
title: "co-orientation loop"
kind: protocol-loop
status: active
updated: 2026-07-11

purpose: >
  Define the universal .yawn loop where a system locates itself, a human or
  observing agent expresses what is present, attributed inference remains
  editable, and proof determines what may become durable.

orientation_layers:
  device:
    knows:
      - runtime
      - protocol
      - coordinate
      - projection
      - available sources
      - permissions
      - blocked effects
  human:
    owns:
      - present expression
      - meaning
      - values
      - consent
      - consequential authorization
  system:
    contributes:
      - attributed inference
      - alternatives
      - confidence
      - unknowns
      - questions
  shared:
    holds:
      - editable working orientation
      - agreement
      - disagreement
      - open questions
      - active coordinate

loop:
  - locate the system
  - receive human expression or another source signal
  - separate observation from inference
  - mirror attributed alternatives and uncertainty
  - keep correction and disagreement visible
  - ask, hold, test, move, or deliberately defer
  - gather proof for specific claims
  - replay what changed
  - update durable memory only when authority and proof permit

state_machines:
  capture:
    - locked
    - armed
    - recording
    - review
    - sending
  orientation:
    - open
    - proposed
    - corrected
    - endorsed
    - evidence-updated
  action:
    - none
    - proposed
    - authorized
    - executing
    - blocked
    - proved
    - failed
  proof:
    - none
    - proposed
    - observed
    - verified
    - contested
    - superseded

movement_rule: >
  Choose the minimum sufficient commitment that can answer the governing
  question. Preserve optionality while uncertainty is high. Bold action may be
  coherent when evidence, authority, downside bounds, and recovery are strong.

projection_rule: >
  Smaller and larger projections share the same orientation revision,
  authority state, and proof state. A projection may omit information but may
  not create a conflicting truth.

boundary:
  - "Human expression and attributed machine inference remain distinguishable."
  - "Shared orientation is a working model, not objective truth."
  - "Non-mutation does not imply privacy or prohibit data egress."
  - "External calls, persistence, publication, spending, and world action require separate authority."
  - "Only proof updates empirical claims in the durable map."

proof:
  condition: >
    A context-free observer can identify every orientation layer, authority
    boundary, active question, proof state, and allowed transition.
  status: contract-defined
